					<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
						Coordinates, Qibla bearing, and solar calculations run entirely on your device. Your
						location never touches a server.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── What is an online compass (SEO / value) ───────── -->
	<section id="about" class="scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow text-center">the free online compass</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-center text-display-lg text-ink">What is an online compass?</h2>
				<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body">
					An <strong class="text-ink font-medium">online compass</strong> is a digital compass
					that runs directly in your web browser — no app to download, no install, and nothing
					to pay for. Open this page on your phone and, in a few seconds, you have a free online
					compass that shows your live heading in degrees, points toward north from wherever you
					are, and can correct itself for the difference between magnetic north and true north.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					Because it lives in the browser, it behaves the same whether you are at home, in the
					field, or in a classroom. Search "online compass" on Google, open this site, and your
					device's sensors take over. Unlike a traditional compass with a needle fighting gravity
					and drift, an online compass combines your phone's magnetometer, gyroscope, and
					accelerometer to produce a more stable and reliable
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">online compass direction</strong>.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					Every tool here is built around one job — helping you orient yourself and
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">find north online</strong> — and around it sits a
					full navigation toolkit: a live heading readout in degrees, cardinal and intercardinal
					directions, a one-tap fullscreen mode, and an accuracy indicator so you always know how
					much to trust the reading. Because everything is processed locally, your location never
					leaves your device.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="mx-auto mt-12 grid max-w-5xl gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
				<div class="card p-6">
					<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">How an online compass works</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
						<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Three sensors</strong> do the work: a
						magnetometer measures Earth's magnetic field, an accelerometer detects which way is
						down, and a gyroscope tracks rotation. The browser fuses them into a single heading,
						and tilt compensation keeps it correct even when the phone is not flat.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card p-6">
					<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Online compass vs. a traditional compass</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
						A needle compass is simple but must sit level, and it always points to magnetic north
						— not the north on your map. An online compass adds the exact degree of your heading,
						automatic declination correction, and a display you can read in the dark.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card p-6">
					<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Why use a browser compass at all?</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
						It opens in one tap from a bookmark or a search, with nothing to install and no
						account. If your phone has a compass app you rarely open, a browser compass is the one
						you will actually use.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── How to use, step by step ───────────────────────── -->
	<section id="how-to-use" class="border-y border-hairline bg-canvas scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-start">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">getting started</p>
					<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">How to use an online compass.</h2>
					<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
						Five steps take you from opening the page to a heading you can trust. The whole
						process takes less than a minute, and you can repeat the calibration whenever the
						reading drifts. For a deeper walkthrough, read our
						<a href="/blog/how-to-use-online-compass/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">step-by-step online compass guide</a>.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 text-sm text-body leading-6">
						The single biggest factor in accuracy is not the software — it is where you stand and
						what is near your phone. Keep that in mind through every step.
					</p>
				</div>
				<ol class="card-soft divide-y divide-hairline">
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">01</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Open the compass on your phone</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Load this page in Safari, Chrome, or Firefox on a phone or tablet with a magnetometer.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">02</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Enable the live compass</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Tap "Enable live compass" and allow motion and orientation access when your browser asks.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">03</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Hold the phone flat, away from metal</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Rest it in your palm, screen up, and keep it clear of laptops, speakers, and magnetic cases.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">04</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Read your heading and calibrate</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">The top marker shows the direction you face. Trace a figure-eight if the number wobbles.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">05</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Switch to true north</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Enable declination so the heading matches maps and any bearing you plan to follow.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── True vs magnetic north ─────────────────────────── -->
	<section id="true-north" class="scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">true vs magnetic north</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Why declination decides whether you're right.</h2>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
					A compass needle does not point to the top of the map. It points to magnetic north, a
					moving spot in the Arctic. The gap between that and true north is declination — the most
					common reason a compass reading seems "wrong".
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					Declination is not a fixed number you can memorise. It depends on where you are on the
					planet, and it drifts by a fraction of a degree each year as Earth's molten core moves.
					In London the two norths have stayed nearly aligned for years, which is why British maps
					often ignore the difference; in parts of Canada and South Africa they can be 20° or more
					apart — the difference between reaching your car and turning down the wrong street.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					This is exactly what an uncorrected compass gets wrong. Our online compass applies the
					World Magnetic Model for your coordinates, so toggling to
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">true north</strong> lines the heading up with a map
					grid. The table below shows roughly how large the correction is in well-known places;
					tap <em>"Use location for declination"</em> on the dial for the exact value where you
					are.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl overflow-x-auto">
				<table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
					<caption class="sr-only">Approximate magnetic declination by city</caption>
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline-strong text-left">
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">Location</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium text-ink">Approximate declination</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{declinationExamples.map((d) => (
							<tr class="border-b border-hairline">
								<td class="py-3 pr-4 text-body">{d.place}</td>
								<td class="py-3 text-body">{d.value}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-mute">
					Approximate mid-2020s values for illustration only. Declination changes every year and
					by exact location — always use the live correction in the tool.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── How to read a compass ──────────────────────────── -->
	<section id="reading" class="border-y border-hairline bg-canvas scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">reading the dial</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Headings, bearings, and the compass rose.</h2>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
					Once the dial is live, the only skill left is reading it.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Heading</h3>
						<p class="text-sm leading-6">The direction you are currently facing, in degrees clockwise from north. Facing east is 90°, south 180°, west 270°.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Bearing</h3>
						<p class="text-sm leading-6">The direction from one point to another. Use the <a href="/bearing/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">bearing calculator</a> to get it between two coordinates.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Azimuth</h3>
						<p class="text-sm leading-6">A bearing expressed as an angle from 0° to 360°. The <a href="/sun/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">sun tool</a> gives the sun's azimuth for your location.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card-soft p-5">
						<h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Back bearing</h3>
						<p class="text-sm leading-6">The reverse direction — the bearing from B back to A. Add 180° if the forward bearing is under 180°, otherwise subtract 180°.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl">
				<h3 class="text-display-sm text-ink">The 16-point compass rose</h3>
				<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Between the four cardinal points sit intermediate points. A 16-point rose divides the
					circle into 22.5° segments — the resolution most navigation and weather reports use.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 sm:grid-cols-4">
					{compassRose.map((c) => (
						<div class="flex items-baseline justify-between border-b border-hairline py-2">
							<span class="font-mono text-sm text-ink">{c.point}</span>
							<span class="font-mono text-xs text-mute">{c.deg}</span>
						</div>
					))}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── Accuracy ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
	<section id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">accuracy</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">How accurate is a phone compass?</h2>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
					Used well, a phone compass is accurate to within a degree or two. Used carelessly, it
					can be off by twenty. The difference is almost entirely environmental.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 text-body leading-7">
					The magnetometer measures field strength in microteslas and is built for navigation, not
					surveying: fine for following a trail or aligning a solar panel, but no match for a
					professional instrument. What matters is the accuracy indicator on the dial — trust a
					steady reading, and move when it jumps by tens of degrees, because the field around you
					is distorted. Indoors, expect a few degrees of drift from wiring and furniture; outdoors,
					clear of a car or railing, readings are reliable. If precision matters, average a few
					readings and cross-check against the
					<a href="/sun/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">sun's position</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── Calibration ────────────────────────────────────── -->
	<section id="calibration" class="border-y border-hairline bg-canvas scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:items-start">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">calibration</p>
					<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Get the most accurate reading.</h2>
					<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
						Phone magnetometers are sensitive to nearby metal and magnetic fields. A few
						seconds of calibration turns a drifting needle into a trustworthy heading.
					</p>
				</div>
				<ol class="card-soft divide-y divide-hairline">
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">01</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Hold the phone flat</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Keep the screen parallel to the ground, in your palm or on a stable surface.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">02</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Trace a figure-eight</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Wave the phone gently in a figure-eight motion a few times, then hold still.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">03</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Move away from interference</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Step away from laptops, phones, magnets, and metal tables before taking a reading.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
					<li class="flex gap-4 p-5">
						<span class="font-mono text-sm text-mute">04</span>
						<div>
							<p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Confirm with the sun</p>
							<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-body">Use the sun position marker on the dial as an independent cross-check in daylight.</p>
						</div>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- ─── Compare compass types ──────────────────────────── -->
	<section id="compare" class="scroll-mt-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
				<p class="eyebrow">compass types</p>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Not all compasses work the same way.</h2>
				<p class="mt-3 text-body-lg text-body">
					Knowing which technology you are holding explains its strengths, its limits, and why a
					phone compass behaves differently from the brass one in a drawer.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mt-10 overflow-x-auto">
				<table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
					<caption class="sr-only">Comparison of compass types</caption>
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline-strong text-left">
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">Type</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">What it measures</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">Best for</th>
							<th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium text-ink">Limitations</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{compassTypes.map((c) => (
							<tr class="border-b border-hairline align-top">
